  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Sitting in Sveta's car, Katya asks Nastya if it is normal to have doubts the night before her wedding. She admits that although Nikita is a good man, she kissed Denis on the roof the very day Nikita proposed.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Вы пока тихо посидите здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Настя, скажи, что мне делать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "А что, мы уже приехали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис предложил с ним уехать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Куда, у тебя завтра свадьба!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо большое, что напомнила мне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это нормально, что я сомневаюсь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Никита ведь очень хороший человек.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, он правда очень хороший.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я изменила ему с Денисом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В тот день он сделал предложение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Всё в порядке, теперь поехали.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Вы пока тихо посидите здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You sit quietly in here for now.' Sveta chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'here' to keep a polite distance. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Настя, скажи, что мне делать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nastya, tell me, what should I do?' Katya brings out the contrast between 'Nastya' and 'say' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "А что, мы уже приехали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, have we arrived already?' Nastya anchors the line on 'and', while 'we've arrived' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис предложил с ним уехать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Denis offered for me to go away with him.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'Denis' and finishing on 'to leave'. Nastya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Куда, у тебя завтра свадьба!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where to, your wedding is tomorrow!' Nastya anchors the line on 'where to', while 'wedding' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо большое, что напомнила мне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you very much for reminding me.' The grammar packs 'thank you' and 'to me' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это нормально, что я сомневаюсь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is it normal that I am having doubts?' Katya anchors the line on 'this', while 'doubt' completes the thought. Nastya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Никита ведь очень хороший человек.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nikita is a very good person, after all.' Nastya brings out the contrast between 'Nikita' and 'after all' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, он правда очень хороший.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, he is truly very good.' Katya anchors the line on 'yes', while 'good' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я изменила ему с Денисом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But I cheated on him with Denis.' The '-a' ending on the past-tense verb for 'would change' marks a female speaker or subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В тот день он сделал предложение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'On that day he proposed to me.' Katya brings out the contrast between 'that' and 'day' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Всё в порядке, теперь поехали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is fine, let us go now.' In Russian, the idea of 'everything' flows naturally into 'here we go' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
